@101 = ~Greetings. Welcome to the Interplanar Emporium. What can I do for you?~
@102 = ~Greetings. We'd like to see your wares, if you please.~ //PXAGENO2
@103 = ~We'd like to use your Magic Cauldron.~
@1031 = ~We'd like to use your Shrinking Chest.~
@1032 = ~Nothing could be simpler. Place a two-handed sword, a longsword, or a katana inside the chest, pay me 500 gold pieces, and I'll activate the machine that will shorten your weapon.~
@10323 = ~Very well. I'll do that. Thank you.~
@10323 = ~Not a bastard sword or a scimitar?~
@10324 = ~No. My chest is calibrated for the most common types of swords.~
@10324 = ~I understand. I'll come back later.~
@104 = ~My Cauldron? There's no need to ask permission. It's self-service, provided you pay me 100 gold pieces for each potion you brew.~
@105 = ~Very well. How am I supposed to know which potions can be combined?~
@106 = ~Ah... that you'll have to discover through experimentation. Or by asking around. As for me, I can only tell you the basics: healing potions and strength potions are very easy to combine, and my Amplifiers can replace the weaker varieties of those potions.~
@107 = ~I see. I'll come back once I've learned a bit more.~
@108 = ~I'll remember that. Now, may I see your wares?~ //MARCHAND
@109 = ~Very well. Thank you.~
@1010 = ~Nothing. We're just looking around.~
@1011 = ~As you wish. Don't hesitate to speak with me if anything catches your eye.~ //EXIT
@1012 = ~I spoke with your familiar. So you're a wizard?~ //+1046
@1013 = ~I thought my robes made that rather obvious. Yes, I am a wizard. Surprised?~~I thought my robes made that rather obvious. Yes, I am a wizard. Surprised?~
@1014 = ~How is that possible? I thought dwarves were incapable of wielding arcane magic.~
@1015 = ~That is my curse. But I don't know you well enough to speak of it.~
@1016 = ~I understand. Perhaps another time.~ //EXIT
@1017 = ~That is my curse. Would you truly like to hear my story?~
@1018 = ~Yes, please.~
@1019 = ~Is it a long story?~
@1020 = ~Quite.~
@1021 = ~Then perhaps another time.~ //EXIT
@1022 = ~I was not always a dwarf. I was born an elf, and believe me, it suited me very well. From an early age I showed an exceptional gift for magic, and I was all the more remarkable because I possessed an affinity for Entropy. Magic fascinated me, and once I had received all the training Semberloge had to offer, I set out to explore the world, determined to gather every scrap of knowledge I could find.~
@1023 = ~It was in the foothills of Lamedragon that I made the greatest and most perilous discovery of my life: an entropic zone. Within one of the caverns enveloped by it lay an abandoned wizard's refuge, its libraries filled with books beyond imagining. The place was protected, of course, but its wards proved no match for me. I made the refuge my home, resolved to uncover every secret its library contained.~
@1024 = ~I soon discovered that the entropic zone amplified my powers. What fortune! A nexus of power, an endless source of knowledge, and a sanctuary all in one. I thought myself blessed. But my joy was short-lived. I quickly realized the zone also magnified wild surges. That troubled me, though not enough to make me cautious. I paid dearly for my arrogance the day an especially violent surge altered my destiny forever. That was the day I became a dwarf.~
@1025 = ~In time I exhausted all the knowledge hidden within the lost library. Finding no remedy for my condition, I resorted to a wish. The genie who granted it twisted my words, giving me the grace and frailty of an elf without restoring my elven body. So I resumed my wanderings. After many long years and countless encounters, when no one on Toril could undo a transformation born of such chaotic magic, I turned my search to other planes.~
@1026 = ~I wandered through many planes. I traveled to Arborea seeking the aid of the Seldarine, yet no one there would tell me where they could be found. I journeyed to Elysium and humbly begged to be healed, but I was judged unworthy of such a blessing. I went to Ysgard hoping Sharindlar might help me, only to be told she would never aid a false dwarf. I even sought out Azuth in Arcadia, but was informed that he had no time to spare for a deluded planar traveler.~
@1027 = ~At last I steeled myself to seek the one thing in all the multiverse that knows every answer and never refuses to share them for the proper price: the Pillar of Skulls. I underestimated the dangers of Baator and was forced to turn back. Since then I have all but abandoned hope. Travel no longer brings me joy, for every journey has become little more than another desperate search for a cure. I called upon the many acquaintances I had made over the years and returned to Toril, settling here in the City of Coin, where the curiosities I import from other planes sell quite well. But to what end? What use is all the wealth I have amassed? It will never be enough to fulfill my one true dream.~
@1028 = ~I don't understand. Why does being a dwarf trouble you so much?~
@1029 = ~That is a sad tale indeed. I would gladly offer my help, but if all your power and all your travels have failed to find a cure, what hope do I have?~
@1030 = ~An interesting story. You did mention that you have little use for your wealth...~
@1031 = ~It is difficult to explain. Pauses thoughtfully. Have you ever been forced against your will to change your body... or even your sex?~
@1032 = ~Then remember how deeply unsettling that experience was. Now imagine living for millennia in a single form, shaped by a single culture, only to have it all torn away. Add to that the knowledge that your own people look upon you with pity, contempt, or ridicule behind polite smiles. Take that feeling of alienation... and multiply it tenfold.~
@1033 = ~That is the best way I can describe it. It is a constant sense that something is profoundly unnatural, coupled with exile from one's own people. I would not wish such a fate upon anyone.~
@1034 = ~I understand. I truly would like to help you but how?~
@1035 = ~Thank you for explaining. I'll leave you to your business.~
@1036 = ~Thank you for sharing your story. I'll return another time.~
@1037 = ~I did say that. But don't mistake me I have no intention of simply giving it away. Interested in my fortune? Then help me.~
@1038 = ~Very well. What must I do?~
@1039 = ~Why bother? I could simply kill you and take it all.~
@1040 = ~You do not threaten me in my own establishment, young man.~~You do not threaten me in my own establishment, young lady.~ //EJECT outside
@1041 = ~I've searched for a cure myself for many years, and in every corner of the multiverse. You are unlikely to discover what I could not. Still, I know you travel far and wide, so I ask only that you keep your eyes open. If you ever find a way to restore me to my elven form, I beg you to return here and tell me.~
@1042 = ~I have searched for a cure myself for many years, and in every corner of the multiverse. You are unlikely to discover what I could not. Still, I know you travel far and wide, so I ask only that you keep your eyes open. If you ever find a way to restore me to my elven form, I beg you to return here and tell me.~
@1043 = ~Very well. I'll do that.~
@1044 = ~And what do I gain from this?~
@1045 = ~I cannot say just yet. But believe me, if you make my dream a reality, you will find me more than generous.~
@1046 = ~I spoke with your familiar. Not the friendliest creature, I must say. She told me you're a wizard?~
@1047 = ~I found this. I hope it may be of some help to you. (Give Elven Transformation Draught.)~
@1048 = ~I found this. I hope it may be of some help to you. (Give Elven Metamorphosis scroll.)~
@1049 = ~It worked! Beaming with delight.~
@1050 = ~I'm glad it met your expectations. Now then... about my reward?~~I'm glad it met your expectations. Now then... about my reward?~
@1051 = ~Breaks into a broad smile as he reads the scroll. This is exactly what I needed! Where did you find it? A tenth-circle spell? Such a thing truly exists? It must date back to the days of ancient Netheril. I won't be able to memorize it, but that hardly matters I shall need to cast it only once. Wish me luck.~
@1052 = ~Ah! Now there is a handsome elf!~
@1053 = ~I can never thank you enough for what you've done. I'll be closing the shop for a while and returning to Semberloge to show my family that I have finally been cured. We'll meet again in a few days. Until then, accept these gifts: a pouch of gold, one of my personal possessions which should serve you as well as it served me and a secret I reveal only to my closest friends: try casting Melf's Acid Arrow into the Cauldron.~
@1054 = ~Very well. Go ahead.~
@1055 = ~Smiles broadly as you explain what the potion is supposed to do. Well then, let us hope it lives up to its promise.~
@1056 = ~I have nothing left to sell you.~
@1057 = ~I should tell you that the spell we used on you was cast through one of the spells you sell yourself.~
@1058 = ~Looks utterly bewildered. What are you saying? The answer has been right before my eyes all this time? And what is the name of this miraculous spell?~
@1059 = ~Bakanëo's Fantastic Rupture.~
@1060 = ~Ah... that spell. Yes, I know it well. But I never dared use it. It amplifies entropic rifts, and after what happened to me...~
@1061 = ~What a shame... If only you had known...~
@1062 = ~Hmph. All those years wasted because you were afraid to use a spell that grants access to every form of magic.~
@1063 = ~Certainly. I have a wide selection of wares. Would you like to browse my magical items, spell scrolls, or my potions and charms?~
@1064 = ~Magical items.~
@1065 = ~Spell scrolls.~
@1066 = ~Potions and charms.~
@1067 = ~Greetings. We'd like to see your magical items.~
@1068 = ~Greetings. We'd like to see the spell scrolls you have for sale.~
@1069 = ~Greetings. We'd like to see the potions you have available.~